From e13a5206e8d58aa037b5836fc6d869f68d109540 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 07:54:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: arm64: clear the right field in rt_sigreturn

sys_rt_sigreturn() cleared PT_REGS_ORIG_SYSCALL to stop the restart
machinery acting on a restored context. That is the wrong field here.
arch/um/kernel/signal.c decides whether a syscall is in flight with
"PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR(regs) >= 0", which is uml_pt_regs.syscall.

x86 writes PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR at this point and has no choice, because
there ORIG_SYSCALL aliases AX and AX is also the return register. arm64
deliberately separated the two, putting ORIG_SYSCALL on a synthetic slot so
that a syscall's first argument survives the return value being seeded.
Translating the line by name therefore moved it to different storage and
left uml_pt_regs.syscall holding __NR_rt_sigreturn.

The result is silent. A handler returning into a context whose x0 holds one
of the four -ERESTART* values, with a second signal deliverable -- which is
the ordinary back-to-back signal case, since set_current_blocked()
recalculates pending signals -- has its context rewritten: the program
counter rewound by four bytes and x0 reloaded from a slot sigreturn never
restored. It corrupts only the guest thread's own registers, and only on a
narrow value match, which is why 550 consecutive clean gate runs did not
find it.

Found by audit, not by a failure.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/arm64/um/signal.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/signal.c b/arch/arm64/um/signal.c
index 4aa7b647f927f..ea5432f924c73 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/um/signal.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/signal.c
@@ -295,8 +295,29 @@ SYSCALL_DEFINE0(rt_sigreturn)
 	if (restore_altstack(&uc->uc_stack))
 		goto segfault;
 
-	/* Do not let the syscall restart machinery act on this return value. */
-	PT_REGS_ORIG_SYSCALL(&current->thread.regs) = -1;
+	/*
+	 * Do not let the syscall restart machinery act on this return value.
+	 *
+	 * It is PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR that has to be cleared, not
+	 * PT_REGS_ORIG_SYSCALL. arch/um/kernel/signal.c tests
+	 * "PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR(regs) >= 0" to decide whether a syscall is in
+	 * flight, and that is uml_pt_regs.syscall -- a different field.
+	 *
+	 * x86 writes PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR here and can write nothing else,
+	 * because there ORIG_SYSCALL aliases AX, which is also the return
+	 * register. On arm64 the two were deliberately separated, ORIG_SYSCALL
+	 * onto a synthetic slot, so translating the line by name moved it to
+	 * different storage and left uml_pt_regs.syscall holding
+	 * __NR_rt_sigreturn.
+	 *
+	 * The consequence is silent: a handler returning into a context whose
+	 * x0 happens to hold one of the four -ERESTART* values, with another
+	 * signal deliverable, gets its context rewritten -- the program counter
+	 * rewound four bytes and x0 replaced from a slot sigreturn never
+	 * restored. Rare enough that 550 clean gate runs did not hit it, and
+	 * entirely invisible when it does.
+	 */
+	PT_REGS_SYSCALL_NR(&current->thread.regs) = -1;
 	return PT_REGS_SYSCALL_RET(&current->thread.regs);
 
  segfault:
